## What was done

**Audit** (`.devin/SEC03-report.md`): inventoried everything Arora persists. Two real credential stores were plaintext: `autofill.dat` (saved form passwords, raw QDataStream) and `proxy/password` in QSettings. WebEngine profile cookies are Chromium OSCrypt-encrypted (obfuscation-class on Linux without a keyring); history/bookmarks/session stay plaintext by design, same as Chrome.

**New `src/securestore.{h,cpp}`**: AES-256-GCM seal/open over OpenSSL EVP, resolved at runtime via `QLibrary` — no OpenSSL dev headers exist on this box, same dlopen pattern Qt's own TLS backend uses. Blob format `ARSEC1|nonce|tag|ct`; 256-bit key in `securestore.key`, forced 0600.

**autofill.dat**: now sealed, atomic `QSaveFile` write at 0600, legacy plaintext files migrate on next save. Fail-secure: if the crypto backend is unavailable, password-bearing forms are never written back as plaintext.

**Proxy password**: sealed as `arsec1:` base64 in QSettings, transparent migration at both read sites (settings dialog, NAM).

