**What was done:**
- **Root cause**: `AdBlockNetwork::match()` walked all rules linearly per network request — ~500ms/lookup at EasyList scale (~60k rules) on the Chromium IO thread.
- **Fix**: `AdBlockRule` now derives a `matchToken` — the longest ASCII literal run between `*^|` metacharacters. Since every run char is a literal atom in the compiled regex, the token is a proven necessary substring of any matching URL. `rebuildRules` builds a per-subscription `RuleIndex` (4-gram hash → token → rule indices) alongside a generic bucket for untokenizable rules (regex, sub-4-char, non-ASCII runs) and a `pageRules` bucket for document-level modifiers. `match()` checks only generic + token-hit candidates in original order — semantics byte-identical.
- **Correctness**: the old linear scan is kept verbatim as `matchLinear()`; a new `differential()` test asserts identical decisions (action/redirect/removeParams) across rules × urls × first-parties × resource types.
- **Measured**: ~500ms → ~0.9ms per request (**~550x**, target was ≥10x); residual is the honest ~2% generic bucket.

